reboot -- Command

Halt the system; this machine has no warm restart
/etc/reboot [ -p ]

/etc/reboot exists under the name an operator will type, and the first thing
it does is tell the truth about it: THE COMMODORE 900 CANNOT RESTART ITSELF IN
SOFTWARE.  It prints

    reboot: this machine cannot restart itself in software.
    reboot: halting instead -- press reset to restart.

and execs /etc/halt, which unmounts what it can, syncs every filesystem so
that none of them comes back up dirty, and stops the machine.  You then press
the reset button, or switch the power off and on again.  With -p it first
prompts and lets you interrupt; without it, it goes.

Only root can halt the machine, so only root can usefully run this.

Why there is no warm restart
There are four reasons and they are all hardware or kernel, not a missing
option to look for:

    There is no reset port.  The boot ROM is fixed read-only at physical
    address 0 and there is no overlay or bank register that could switch it
    back in over the running system; nothing in the ROM's I/O map is a reset
    line.

    Z8001 reset is a PIN EVENT, not an instruction.  It also resets the Z8010
    MMU and the peripherals, which a jump into the ROM would not, so the ROM
    would start executing with the running system's segment mapping still in
    force and would not survive its first memory reference.

    The one restart interface the ROM declares is never filled in.  struct
    romconf has an `int *rom_restart', but configure() assigns only rom_bram,
    rom_eram and rom_ctype and leaves the rest zero -- so calling through it
    would be a call to segment 0, offset 0.

    Neither donor reboot works here.  The COHERENT 3.x /etc/reboot pulses the
    8042 keyboard controller's reset line (ATrebootas.s), which is
    PC/AT hardware this machine does not have, and it is not built.  The 4.x
    one asks the kernel with cohcall(COH_REBOOT), and this kernel has no such
    system call.

So a `reboot' that appeared to work would only be a halt that did not say so,
and the failure mode of that is an operator who walks away from a machine that
is not coming back.

Notes
This page REPLACES the COHERENT 4.2 Lexicon article of the same name, which
describes a machine that can restart itself.  On this hardware that article
documents a command that cannot exist, so it is not shipped; nothing of Mark
Williams Company's text remains in what you are reading.

/etc/reboot is the single-user shortcut and warns nobody.  For an orderly
shutdown with a countdown and a message to every logged-in user, use
/etc/shutdown.  `shutdown reboot' is accepted and is not a lie about a warm
restart: it records the level, and /.profile then says the machine cannot
restart itself and halts it.

